  "map_verdict": "The sorted-node map the RE-015 flag-group chain stores into is owned by the large consumer 0x15a3b90 itself. Its 24-byte handle {root, height, size} lives in the consumer's frame at [rsp+0x178]: the consumer zeroes the root and the size at the head of every field-loop iteration, and never stores the height word at all. The handle never arrives from a caller or a field -- the consumer's only direct caller (the wrapper FDE at 0x15a6510) passes an out scratch, a record pointer, and a scalar. The insert family writes the handle only through the pointer the consumer hands it as the miss record's first word: the insert's creation path writes the fresh root and zeroes the height, the delegate's root split replaces the root and increments the height, and the insert's common exit increments the size. Stored values are read back in three places: the segment walks themselves (the map-hit replacement, traced by the out-buffer-owner packet), a phase-2 probe walk that loads only the found entry's capacity word and compares it against the sentinel -- never the pointer or length, and nothing is copied or freed there -- and the per-iteration drain on the loop's back-edge, where the collect helper 0x153ba90 walks the tree by its count and child offsets and frees the nodes, and the consumer frees each collected element's buffer through the dealloc slot under the 0/sentinel guard. Inside the insert family, splits move stored triples verbatim through the memmove slot and no stored entry is freed: the delegate's six dealloc calls all sit in post-return panic/unwind pads freeing its own in-flight allocations.",
